From c6668ae8dc607445f25fad233f2e797aca4e5690 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Nekokabu <nekokabu@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 22:38:03 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 26/32] n64: an SP DMA costs the RDP more in the megabyte of
 the colour buffer, of the depth buffer or of the VI's picture (rdp_timing
 v57)

rdp_timing v57 (NTSC console; SP DMA started by the CPU beside four game
lists, with the VI off, in the title's VI mode, and in that mode with the
VI showing the megabyte the DMA uses; the VI confirmed running for every
row) measured what a transfer costs the RDP by where its RDRAM side is,
against a megabyte the list does not use:

  megabyte of the colour buffer            +3.0 to 6.2 clocks (4.7)
  megabyte of the depth buffer, lists      +3.5 to 5.1 (4.3)
   that use depth (BAR, DK64)
  megabyte the VI shows, AA modes 0/1      +4.1 to 4.7 for 168 B / 344 B back
                                           to back, +10.3 / 12.0 for 1024 B,
                                           +6.5 to 10.4 after a pause
  the same in the MGC demo's AA mode 2     +1.9 to 2.4, +4.3 / 4.8, +1.5 to 3.5

The first two grow with the VI like the transfer itself (x1.15 to 1.27 at
320x240, back-to-back transfers included; patch 0025); the VI's term is
stated in clocks of that VI mode.

In the games these places are common: the Beetle Adventure Racing intro
reads 21% of its transfers from the colour buffer's megabyte, 38% from the
depth buffer's and 21% from the one the VI shows; a third of the MGC
demo's output is written to the colour buffer's megabyte and a third to
the one the VI shows; DK64 writes its output to the depth buffer's
megabyte.

dmaContention() adds the terms to the first row of a transfer. Whether the
list uses the depth buffer is taken per frame (a depth primitive seen in
this frame or the one before it).

Results in the test harness (series v21 against v20): DK64 title demo
frame deltas 2 / 3 / 4+ = 8 / 381 / 33, 46 frames differing from the
console (was 6 / 381 / 36, 51; console 9 / 385-391 / 25). BAR intro 253 /
326 frames in the two segments (was 255 / 327; console 231 / 325). MGC
2011 demo: parts 1 to 5 -0.1 / +0.2 / +0.0 / -0.4 / +1.2%, the "@" frame at
VI 9389 (was 9382; console 9385).
